The above code uses `strtrim` function from `string.h` header file to trim any trailing whitespace characters from the command line argument. The `strtrim` function is defined as follows:
```
char* strtrim(const char* s) {
    int i = 0;
    while (s[i] && !isspace(s[i])) i++;
    s[i] = '\0';
    return s;
}
```
This function takes a string `s` as input and returns a pointer to the trimmed string. The trimming is done by setting the first whitespace character after the non-whitespace characters to `\0`. This way, any trailing whitespace characters are removed from the string.

The main function then checks if there is a command line argument provided using `argc`, and if not, it prints an error message and returns. If there is a command line argument, it trims any trailing whitespace characters using `strtrim` function and prints the trimmed argument to the console using `printf`.']

